{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the days of Israel drew near to die.</b> Wherever 'drawing near' is said, he did not reach the days of his fathers. So too 'the days of David drew near' — he did not reach the days of Boaz, and Obed, and Jesse, who lived more than four hundred years, while David lived only seventy years." "<b>And do with me kindness and truth.</b> That is to say: you must say that you are obligated to bury me in the land of Canaan; therefore I entreat you to do with me kindness and truth, and take the trouble to bury me there." "<b>Pray do not bury me in Egypt.</b> What Rashi explained — because the dust of the land of Egypt is destined to become lice — does not seem right to me, for from this he would have no reason to fear, since now Binyamin his son was not afflicted by the louse, all the more so his father — unless you say that Jacob did not know this. And Rabbenu Tam says that he feared lest the Egyptians be saved from the plague of the firstborn on his account if he were buried in Egypt, for Israel is called a scattered sheep, and Egyptians are called donkeys, as it is said: ״אשר בשר חמורים בשרם״, and it is written: ״פטר חמור תפדה בשה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:07:21.644447966Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}